analysis:
Both changed files are test-only: StatusCommandRunTests.swift (new lateLeaseRenewalStillAuthorizes, head lines 280-305) and ProviderAuthorizationReadinessTests.swift (new display/removal tests, head lines 100-260, updated summary() call sites). No production code is in this batch, so no direct security regression is visible. The tests encode a split between display and removal decisions, which matters for TB-011, T-053, T-057 and A-016.

Display (displayedStatus / displayedProviderAuthorization) keeps showing App Attest authorization while the lease is unexpired and the daemon snapshot is fresh, even when the renewal is older than snapshotMaxAge. Removal (currentStatus / removalReady) still requires a receipt within the 10 s window, an online status, a matching process and a matching coordinator. A late lease then shows 'needs fresh coordinator readiness' and 'keep existing management profiles installed', never 'removal is available' or 'unenroll'. Tests also cover:
- expiresAt boundaries, NaN and infinite expiry;
- a removal decision from a different session, machine, reason or protocol version cannot enable removal;
- a removal decision cannot restore a missing or expired display;
- an untrusted status, a mismatched coordinator or process identity, and a stale writtenAt all yield nil;
- the lease cannot outlive a daemon that stopped writing its snapshot.
These assertions are conservative: the fail-safe direction (no MDM removal without fresh readiness) is preserved.

A test prints a witness line (APP_ATTEST_LATE_LEASE_WITNESS) containing only the summary, with no secrets. The status test runs in a sandboxed child process.

The production implementations (ProviderAuthorizationReadiness.displayedStatus/summary, DaemonState.displayedProviderAuthorization, Status command) were not supplied, so I could not verify that they match these test expectations. In particular, the next pass should check that the display path is only informational and never feeds the unenroll or MDM-removal gating, and that the daemon-written state-file lease cannot be forged by a local same-UID process to induce a misleading 'authorized' display. The display is a diagnostic and is not serving authorization.
